+projected onto the fit, and every door branch of the run is proposed again
+against the fitted run, preferring one that meets it within 10 degrees of a
+right angle (the join slides along the run). A branch that cannot be proposed
+again keeps its exact checked approach and join, with one short lane piece to
+the run.
+
+A run is replaced only if every new run piece, every changed neighbouring
+lane piece and every moved door branch passes the same exact support, the
+complete network (all lanes and door branches) gains no topology contact (a
+contact is identified by its approach-conflict key and its exact rational
+location, so a run may keep a contact the network already had but never add
+or move one),
+and no required original edge's shared-lane route grows by more than 15 % plus
+3 ft. Otherwise that run keeps its checked geometry and the next run is tried.
+Loops, islands and genuine corners stay; terminals, endpoints and ownership are
+unchanged. `--without-straight-runs` reproduces the previous compiler as a
+separately bound experiment. Each network's `statistics().straightRuns` records
+fitted and kept runs (by reason), pieces, moved junctions, re-proposed and
+perpendicular door branches. If a cell needs legacy fallback guides (built
+from another network) and they cross its straight-run guides more than they
+cross the guides compiled without straight runs, the cell is compiled without
+straight runs and its report records `straightRunsWithdrawn`.
